    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "section_1",
        "narration": "For years, React developers suffered through lifecycle soup. You wrote setup logic in componentDidMount, duplicate teardown in componentWillUnmount, and synced prop changes inside componentDidUpdate. One logical feature was chopped into three distinct places, bound together by chaotic this bindings and wrapper hell. <break time='0.6s'/> React Hooks didn't just shorten your code — they completely revolutionized React by decoupling stateful logic from class lifecycles. Instead of grouping code by when it runs, hooks let you group code by what it actually does. But behind that clean functional syntax lies a persistent array structure in Fiber nodes that trips up almost every developer. Understanding this hidden mechanism is the exact difference between fighting stale renders and mastering React.",
        "objective": "",
