      {
        "id": "section_3",
        "narration": "This pointer-based memory model explains React's most frustrating bug: the stale closure. When a hook like useEffect or useCallback captures a variable, it closes over the exact value from that render pass. If your dependency array is missing a value, that closure stays frozen in time, reading snapshot values from the past. <break time='0.6s'/> You don't fix this by disabling ESLint warnings or blindly wrapping every function in useMemo. You fix it by passing functional updates like setCount prev to prev plus 1 or syncing via custom hooks. Respect the Fiber execution queue, keep your dependencies honest, and you will never debug a mystery render again.",
        "objective": "",
