      {
        "id": "section_2_v1",
        "type": "CodeEditorScene",
        "title": "The Golden Rule of Hooks",
        "subtitle": "Never call hooks inside conditions",
        "narration": "Here is the core truth: React components are just JavaScript functions that re-run from scratch on every single render. So where does useState actually keep your data when the component function finishes executing? <break time='0.6s'/> React attaches a hidden linked list directly to your component's internal Fiber node in memory. Every single time a hook runs, React steps forward through that list using an internal index pointer. This is why the Rules of Hooks exist. When you call useState conditionally inside an if block, you shift the execution indices for every single hook that follows. React doesn't identify your state by variable names — it identifies state strictly by call order. Shift that order, and your state quietly cross-contaminates. Once you realize hooks rely on pointer offsets, early returns and conditional hooks suddenly make complete sense.",
        "actualDurationSec": 0,
        "estimatedDurationSec": 0,
        "payload": {
          "engine": "",
          "inputs": {},
          "layout": "fullscreen",
          "code": "function UserProfile({ userId }) {\n  // ❌ BAD: Conditional hook breaks index order!\n  if (userId) {\n    const [user] = useState(null);\n  }\n  \n  // Fiber memoizedState pointer gets desynchronized!\n  const [theme] = useState('dark');\n}",
